// External Package & npm in Node.js

1. What is an External Package
An external package is a reusable library created by others and not built into Node.js.
It adds extra features like colors, frameworks, utilities, etc.

Example: colors, express, lodash

2. How to Install External Package
External packages are installed using npm (Node Package Manager).
Command used in terminal:
npm install colors
npm i colors

// Uses in node
const colors = require("colors");
console.log("Hello Node".green);
console.log("Error Message".red);


// 4. What is package.json

package.json is a configuration file for a Node.js project.
It stores project details and installed dependencies.
Example content:

{
  "name": "myproject",
  "version": "1.0.0",
  "dependencies": {
    "colors": "^1.4.0"
  }
}

// Uses of package.json:
Manages project information
Tracks installed packages
Helps recreate the project using npm install


// package-lock.json in Node.js

What it is:
package-lock.json is automatically generated when you install npm packages. It locks the exact version of every installed package and its dependencies.

Purpose:
Ensures that the project works the same on every machine.
Prevents issues caused by automatic updates of dependencies.
Improves installation speed because npm knows the exact versions to fetch.

// Difference from package.json:
package.json lists direct dependencies with version ranges.
package-lock.json records exact versions of all dependencies and sub-dependencies.